WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:05.588
I think I have a missionary zeal
to bring culture closer to young people.

00:00:05.588 --> 00:00:10.552
Being exposed to culture:  theater,
novels, films,  makes us better thinkers

00:00:10.552 --> 00:00:13.638
and helps us understand
the problems of our world.

00:00:13.638 --> 00:00:18.101
My name is Fatima Naqvi and I'm
a professor of German and film studies.

00:00:18.101 --> 00:00:22.397
I am interested in the way we move in  built space

00:00:22.397 --> 00:00:24.983
and how that built space influences us.

00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:27.777
And my new project is on hospitals.

00:00:27.777 --> 00:00:32.073
I started becoming interested in hospitals
as they were being built in the 1880s

00:00:32.073 --> 00:00:33.908
in the Austro-Hungarian Empire.

00:00:33.908 --> 00:00:39.956
And at that time, Austria-Hungary
was the epicenter of modern medicine.

00:00:39.956 --> 00:00:41.916
They're thinking about every aspect:

00:00:41.916 --> 00:00:45.712
ventilation, what kind of windows,
what kind of paint do you use on the walls,

00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:50.675
wheras the late 20th century is very much
about all the stuff that's underground,

00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:54.512
the hallways that are endless,
the bureaucracy that has to be navigated.

00:00:54.512 --> 00:00:58.141
I was very interested in looking at
the way in which artists reflected on

00:00:58.141 --> 00:01:00.226
those architectural advances.

00:01:01.478 --> 00:01:05.857
I start with people like Arthur Schnitzler
and Rainer Maria Rilke.

00:01:05.857 --> 00:01:09.652
I move through the 20th century via
authors like Thomas Bernhard

00:01:09.652 --> 00:01:13.031
and then I actually look at filmmakers
in the 21st century

00:01:13.031 --> 00:01:16.993
who work on this interface
of documentary and fiction.

00:01:16.993 --> 00:01:21.414
There's a fantastic documentary
by Nikolaus Geyrhalter

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:25.251
about one of these large hospitals
that has been recently built in Vienna.

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:29.255
He starts us off in the basement
where these robots are transporting

00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:34.135
dirty laundry and the refuse
that's left over at the end of the day.

00:01:34.135 --> 00:01:37.138
Watching this film,
you realize that there's so much of it

00:01:37.138 --> 00:01:40.016
that happens behind closed doors
and underground,

00:01:40.016 --> 00:01:43.812
this whole machine hospital complex

00:01:43.812 --> 00:01:48.149
to which we're not really privy, but which determines
our experience once we're in those walls.

00:01:48.149 --> 00:01:51.069
It shows us a world
where there's no daylight, 

00:01:51.069 --> 00:01:53.321
and it leads also to reflect upon

00:01:53.321 --> 00:01:56.407
 what it might mean
for health and recovery

00:01:56.407 --> 00:01:59.953
to be in these
completely artificial spaces.

00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:04.624
A lot of films started to deal with this
in light of Europe's aging population.

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:06.334
And we saw it with the pandemic.

00:02:06.334 --> 00:02:10.088
We were bombarded
with images of people in ICUs.

00:02:10.088 --> 00:02:13.675
These last few years have shown us
how hospitals

00:02:13.675 --> 00:02:15.885
are a very fraught topic for us.

00:02:15.885 --> 00:02:19.472
What does it mean for the end of our lives
that we spend these

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:24.435
in institutional settings where we are
very cut off from the outside?

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:29.107
Most of my work arises
out of conversations with people.

00:02:29.107 --> 00:02:29.983
The wonderful thing

00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:34.362
about Yale is that the colleagues are so willing
to listen to each other,

00:02:34.362 --> 00:02:38.366
throw ideas around, to be very open
and very curious.

00:02:38.366 --> 00:02:40.702
And the Yale School of Architecture
is here.

00:02:40.702 --> 00:02:44.998
People there have helped me
think about space in a new way.

00:02:44.998 --> 00:02:50.920
As I then encounter particular works of art, books
that I'm reading, films that I'm viewing, some of them

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:54.757
start to speak in a new way then,
and I want to dig deeper.

00:02:54.757 --> 00:03:00.597
And that usually does entail going
to the archives, looking at manuscripts, storyboards,

00:03:00.597 --> 00:03:03.099
to see what kind of notes
are there in the margins,

00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:07.604
what things tell us beyond the printed page
or the completed project.

00:03:07.604 --> 00:03:13.109
I am hoping to make people more aware
of their material surroundings,

00:03:13.109 --> 00:03:18.406
to think more profoundly about what kind of spaces
we are creating for ourselves,

00:03:18.406 --> 00:03:21.534
for our children, for the future.

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:23.453
Whether you're looking at a building

00:03:23.453 --> 00:03:25.622
or a film or

00:03:25.622 --> 00:03:27.081
a billboard,

00:03:27.081 --> 00:03:30.001
everything around us in human culture

00:03:30.001 --> 00:03:33.087
can be read and interpreted.

00:03:33.087 --> 00:03:35.757
And I think that we have to return to them
over and over again

00:03:35.757 --> 00:03:39.010
to be able to finally really see them
and understand them.

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:42.555
That's not something that you
can do with a quick glance.

